WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.280
Alright everyone, so now we're going to focus on a very important use case.

2
00:00:07.280 --> 00:00:13.640
One of the must-know use cases for data aggregations is doing running total and rolling total.

3
00:00:13.640 --> 00:00:18.639
These two concepts are very important for data analysis and doing reporting that you must know.

4
00:00:18.639 --> 00:00:22.680
The key use case for those two concepts is to do tracking.

5
00:00:22.680 --> 00:00:28.479
For example, we can go and track the current total sales with the target sales in our business,

6
00:00:28.479 --> 00:00:32.759
and as well, it's great in order to do historical analysis for the trends.

7
00:00:32.759 --> 00:00:36.479
Okay, so now the question is, what is running and rolling total?

8
00:00:36.479 --> 00:00:37.479
They are basically very similar.

9
00:00:37.479 --> 00:00:41.680
They're going to go and aggregate a sequence of members, and the aggregation is going to

10
00:00:41.680 --> 00:00:45.319
get updated each time we add a new member to the sequence.

11
00:00:45.319 --> 00:00:47.880
A sequence could be like a time sequence.

12
00:00:47.880 --> 00:00:51.119
That's why we call this type an analysis of our time.

13
00:00:51.119 --> 00:00:56.040
So now we still have the question, what is the difference between the running and the rolling totals?

14
00:00:56.040 --> 00:01:00.560
The running total can go and aggregate everything from the beginning until the current data

15
00:01:00.560 --> 00:01:06.120
point without dropping off any old data, where on the other hand, in the rolling total, it's

16
00:01:06.120 --> 00:01:12.040
going to go and focus on a specific time window, like the last 30 days or the last two months,

17
00:01:12.040 --> 00:01:16.879
and each time we add a new member or a new data point to the window, we will be dropping

18
00:01:16.879 --> 00:01:19.839
off the oldest data point in the window.

19
00:01:19.839 --> 00:01:24.160
And with this, we're going to get the effect of rolling or let's say shifting window.

20
00:01:24.279 --> 00:01:26.720
Okay, I totally understand if this might be complicated.

21
00:01:26.720 --> 00:01:30.480
Now let's go and have a very simple example in order to understand this concept and as

22
00:01:30.480 --> 00:01:32.480
well how we can solve it using SQL.

23
00:01:32.480 --> 00:01:33.480
All right, guys.

24
00:01:33.480 --> 00:01:34.480
So now we have a simple example.

25
00:01:34.480 --> 00:01:38.879
We have the months and sales, and we have it twice because I want to show you side by

26
00:01:38.879 --> 00:01:42.839
side how SQL works with the running total and the rolling total.

27
00:01:42.839 --> 00:01:44.400
So now what is the task on the left side?

28
00:01:44.400 --> 00:01:49.720
We want to find the running total of sales for each month, and on the right side, we

29
00:01:49.720 --> 00:01:54.639
would like to find three month rolling total of the sales for each month.

30
00:01:54.639 --> 00:01:58.839
So they sound very similar, but on the right side, we have only fixed window.

31
00:01:58.839 --> 00:02:03.760
So now how we can solve this using SQL on the left side, we can use sum of sales.

32
00:02:03.760 --> 00:02:07.919
So we want to go and aggregate all the sales using the sum function and the definition

33
00:02:07.919 --> 00:02:10.679
for the window going to be like this order by month.

34
00:02:10.679 --> 00:02:14.080
And of course you can go and do anything like you can have here an average.

35
00:02:14.080 --> 00:02:18.360
And if you use an average with order by, you will get the running average or the running

36
00:02:18.360 --> 00:02:20.919
max or the running count and so on.

37
00:02:20.919 --> 00:02:24.919
So that means always if you go and mix an aggregate function together with an order

38
00:02:24.919 --> 00:02:28.720
by, you will generate an effect of running total.

39
00:02:28.720 --> 00:02:30.880
Now on the right side, we're going to have the same stuff.

40
00:02:30.880 --> 00:02:33.960
So we're going to have an aggregate function together with order by.

41
00:02:33.960 --> 00:02:36.479
So sum of sales, order by month.

42
00:02:36.479 --> 00:02:38.720
So far we have everything like the left side, right?

43
00:02:38.720 --> 00:02:42.919
But now you might ask, why is SQL going to go and generate this effect, the running total?

44
00:02:42.919 --> 00:02:45.720
We didn't specify like crazy stuff, right?

45
00:02:45.720 --> 00:02:48.320
It's all about the definition of the frame close.

46
00:02:48.320 --> 00:02:53.119
So now do you remember if you use an order by and you don't specify a frame close, you

47
00:02:53.119 --> 00:02:56.479
will get like hidden or let's say default frame close.

48
00:02:56.479 --> 00:03:01.320
And it's going to look like this rows between unbounded proceeding and current row.

49
00:03:01.320 --> 00:03:03.479
And what was the definition of the running total?

50
00:03:03.479 --> 00:03:08.240
It's going to go and aggregate all the data from the very first beginning, well, the unbounded

51
00:03:08.240 --> 00:03:14.240
proceeding until the current position, the current row without dropping off any old members.

52
00:03:14.240 --> 00:03:18.199
So that means that the definition of the running total is going to be the exact definition

53
00:03:18.199 --> 00:03:20.479
of the default frame close.

54
00:03:20.479 --> 00:03:24.000
That's why it's going to go and generate the effect of the running total.

55
00:03:24.000 --> 00:03:26.759
Now let's go to the right side, the rolling total.

56
00:03:26.759 --> 00:03:28.600
Here again, we have the same stuff, right?

57
00:03:28.600 --> 00:03:32.479
We're going to go and aggregate the data using the sum function, and we're going to go and

58
00:03:32.479 --> 00:03:34.399
store the data order by month.

59
00:03:34.399 --> 00:03:38.160
So with that, we are as well generating the effect of running total.

60
00:03:38.160 --> 00:03:40.800
So each time you use order by with aggregate function.

